## The trait

```rust
pub enum EngineKind { Claude, Codex, Git }

pub enum Availability { Present(PathBuf), Missing }

pub trait Engine: Send + Sync {          // Sync: &Engine is shared read-only
                                          // across std::thread::scope workers
    fn kind(&self) -> EngineKind;
    fn bin(&self) -> &str;
    fn default_args(&self) -> &[String];
    /// Cheap PATH probe. Called at DISPATCH time only.
    fn availability(&self) -> Availability;
    /// Returns a VALUE, never a Result. That is the structural guarantee that
    /// one failing repo cannot stop the fleet.
    fn checkpoint(&self, ctx: &EngineContext<'_>) -> EngineOutcome;
}

pub enum EngineOutcome {
    Committed { commits: Vec<CommitRecord> },
    NothingToCommit,
    Failed { error: String, class: FailureClass },
    Unavailable { binary: String, hint: String },
    TimedOut { after: Duration },   // distinct from Failed: a kill is not a refusal
}
```

## Why `EngineOutcome` is a value and not a `Result`

A `Result` invites `?` at some call site inside a worker, and **one `?` that escapes converts a per-repo failure into a fleet abort.** This is the single most load-bearing type decision in the crate.

`Availability` and `TimedOut` exist because a bare `Failed` cannot distinguish a normal error from a killed child — and the whole reason this is a trait rather than an `enum` is that adding a fourth engine must not mean editing every match arm.

## `EngineContext` — the security boundary in the type

```rust
pub struct EngineContext<'a> {
    pub repo_root: &'a Path,
    /// Snapshot in preflight, BEFORE any branch is created and before the
    /// engine runs. An engine may switch branches itself.
    pub base_branch: String,
    pub identity: Option<&'a CommitIdentity>,
    pub timeout: Duration,
    pub message_override: Option<&'a str>,
    /// Merged into the child env — AFTER subtraction, not instead of it.
    pub env: &'a [(String, String)],
}
```

`Send + Sync` is because `&Engine` is shared read-only across `std::thread::scope` workers. The **orchestrator stays in the `ro` binary** — the two-crate split in an earlier draft minted a hard cross-crate boundary between `EngineOutcome` and `RepoOutcome` for very little, and its own trait sketch showed the symptom.

## Exactly three manifest edits, all in the SAME commit

1. root `Cargo.toml` -> `[workspace] members`: add `"crates/ro-engine"`
2. root `Cargo.toml` -> `[workspace.dependencies]`: add `ro-engine = { path = "crates/ro-engine" }`
3. `crates/ro/Cargo.toml` -> `[dependencies]`: add `ro-engine.workspace = true`

`[workspace.metadata.dist]` already sets `precise-builds = true` (publish only the `ro` binary crate), so the release workflow is unaffected.

**No clap and no tokio in ro-engine** — it is a lib. A library that pulls in clap to parse three strings is a library that cannot be used from a plain unit test.
